Z||000000||aubicon||03-03-2001||05:14 PM||||I m overhauling my stering assembly and have ran across a problem my book tells me to use a wrench on the rack and a wrench on the rod assebly so that you can unscrew the rod from the rack, but it apears that the conection is pressed on and not screwed on. any idea of what to do.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||03-03-2001||05:47 PM||||Are you talking about the inner tie-rods? They are threaded on. You can see the flats on the tie-rod and the rack rod. The ends are hammered down to keep them on. You need a crowfoot wrench to turn the tie-rod while holding the rack-rod in place.<P>Because the tie-rod threads are recessed, I can see where you think they are pressed on.<P>Ignore this if it's not what you mean.||||reg||1
Z||000002||aubicon||03-03-2001||06:31 PM||||thanks for the help now to see if i can get these things to turn. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||aubicon||03-03-2001||09:06 PM||||well that worked but now something else is giving me problems, there is a seal around the pinion where the stering columb attaches and i cannot figure out how to get that seal out any idias.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||03-03-2001||09:24 PM||||Let me see if I can ruin your day in two ways here:<P>The seal must be pried out and replaced.<P>If you just have a bad tie-rod, replace that/those but if the pinion to rack mesh is worn, you'll have to replace the complete rack. In the three that I've replaced, that was the main failure.||||reg||1
